.TH "tangsible-debug" "1" "2026-10-19" "Tangsible" "Tangsible Manual"
.PP
.SH NAME
.PP
tangsible-debug - set breakpoints and step through a playbook
.PP
.SH SYNOPSIS
.PP
\fBtangsible debug\fR [\fIplaybook.\&yml\fR] [--vars-at-stops-only] [\fIansible-playbook args\fR .\&.\&.\&]
.PP
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
\fBdebug\fR lets you pause a playbook run at specific tasks, step through it
task by task, and inspect each paused task'\&s own variables - built on
ansible-core'\&s own built-in task debugger, not a separate interactive
protocol of its own.\&
.PP
It never touches the real playbook or role files on disk: \fBdebug\fR first
builds a disposable, hardlinked copy of the whole playbook'\&s directory tree
(under \fB.\&tangsible/debug/\fR next to the playbook, removed again on exit),
splices ansible-core'\&s \fBdebugger: always\fR onto every task in that copy, and
runs the copy instead.\& Every task therefore pauses; a breakpoint you set is
purely a client-side choice of which of those pauses to actually show you -
\fBdebug\fR resumes silently past every other one on your behalf, which is also
why breakpoints can be toggled live, mid-run, with no re-editing of
anything.\&
.PP
On startup, \fBdebug\fR shows every play and task the run will touch, predicted
the same way the live tree'\&s own progress indicator is (a throwaway
\fB--list-tasks --list-hosts\fR probe) - a notified handler, or a dynamically
resolved \fBinclude_tasks:\fR, won'\&t appear in this predicted list even though
it will genuinely run; see \fBtangsible\fR(1)'\&s own \fB--list-tasks\fR caveats.\& The
whole predicted tree is visible up front, before anything has run, so
breakpoints can be armed before the first \fBF5\fR/\fBF10\fR.\&
.PP
\fBdebug\fR is the exact same live tree UI \fBtangsible-run\fR(1) uses, with
breakpoint/pause state layered on top - not a second, separate screen.\&
Every \fBtangsible-run\fR(1) key binding (navigation, Enter-to-expand, the
two-pane drill-down and its tabs, filter, search, recap, \fBq\fR/Ctrl-C) works
identically here; see that page for all of those.\& There is no rerun
dialog and no recorded run history, though - a debug session is not
something a later \fBtangsible rerun\fR can replay.\&
.PP
.SH ADDITIONAL OPTIONS
.PP
\fB--vars-at-stops-only\fR
.RS 4
By default, \fBdebug\fR captures each paused task'\&s own variables for
\fBevery\fR task reached, including one silently resumed past with no
breakpoint armed on it, so its Resolved tab works no matter which
task you later navigate back to - at the cost of paying that
capture'\&s own real time on every single task, whether or not the run
ever actually stops there.\& This flag captures only at a pause
actually shown to you (an armed breakpoint, a step, or a task
failure), trading that for a noticeably faster run through everything
else.\&
.PP
.RE
.SH CONTROLS
.PP
Three keys exist only for this verb, on top of everything \fBtangsible-run\fR(1)
already offers:
.PP
\fBF5\fR
.RS 4
Run: before a run starts, or while paused, resumes past every pause
whose task isn'\&t armed, surfacing only an armed one - or runs to
completion outright if nothing is armed at all.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBF9\fR
.RS 4
Arm or disarm a breakpoint on the selected task row (a no-op on a play
header or a host row) - shown as a red \fB●\fR before the task'\&s name.\& A
left click on that same first column does the same thing, instead of
the row'\&s usual expand/collapse.\&
.PP
.RE
\fBF10\fR
.RS 4
Step: before a run starts, or while paused, stops at the very next
pause unconditionally, armed or not.\&
.PP
.RE
Whenever a pause surfaces (an armed breakpoint, or a step), the drill-down
opens automatically for that exact paused task and host, in whichever of
full-screen or two-pane mode the terminal'\&s current width calls for.\& Its
\fBResolved\fR tab works for \fBany\fR task'\&s drill-down, not just the one
currently paused at - every task'\&s own variables are captured as the run
reaches it (see \fB--vars-at-stops-only\fR above to trade that for speed
instead) - showing the task'\&s source with every variable reference filled
in from its own captured state, including \fBwhen:\fR/\fBfailed_when:\fR/
\fBchanged_when:\fR/\fBuntil:\fR conditions and a \fBregister:\fR target'\&s own result.\&
The raw-output (\fBo\fR) view has nothing to show in a debug session, since the
debugger itself owns the child'\&s stdout for its own prompt text.\&
.PP
\fBq\fR/\fBCtrl-C\fR interrupts the child while it'\&s running or paused, and quits
once it'\&s finished (or immediately, before any run has started) - the same
two-stage behavior \fBtangsible-run\fR(1) already has.\&
.PP
.SH LIMITATIONS
.PP
Deliberately out of scope for now: Step Out (ansible-core has no real call
stack to step out of), modifying a variable'\&s value, and \fB--step\fR (rejected
outright - it would pause the child for a second, unrelated reason \fBdebug\fR
doesn'\&t know how to drive; \fBdebug\fR'\&s own breakpoints are what pause a run).\&
Two tasks sharing the exact same name alias to the same breakpoint.\&
.PP
.SH EXIT STATUS
.PP
Always \fB0\fR once the TUI itself exits normally, regardless of whether any
task actually failed - the tree'\&s own per-host coloring already shows
that.\& \fB2\fR on a usage error (no playbook could be resolved, or \fB--step\fR was
given).\&
.PP
.SH SEE ALSO
.PP
\fBtangsible\fR(1), \fBtangsible-run\fR(1)
